WEBVTT

00:01.800 --> 00:04.878
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.946 --> 00:08.294
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.362 --> 00:11.424
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.509 --> 00:14.420
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.487 --> 00:18.368
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.419 --> 00:21.298
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.384 --> 00:22.905
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:22.973 --> 00:24.278
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.364 --> 00:28.832
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.128 --> 00:39.145
<i>TV ANNOUNCER:
We now return to a later-season
episode of Family Ties.</i>

00:39.213 --> 00:42.659
Elise, have you noticed how
puberty has changed Jennifer?

00:42.726 --> 00:45.136
Shh! Keep
your voice down.

00:45.204 --> 00:47.413
I have a date
with my boyfriend.

00:47.482 --> 00:49.790
Well, uh,
have fun.

00:49.858 --> 00:51.330
You are to gather
grapes for wine

00:51.397 --> 00:53.321
and prepare victuals
for my return.

00:56.116 --> 00:57.455
(phone rings)

00:57.523 --> 00:58.559
Griffin residence.

00:58.628 --> 01:00.268
Oh, hi, Peter.

01:00.335 --> 01:02.073
Who is this? How do
you know my name?

01:02.141 --> 01:03.447
Give me back my son!

01:03.516 --> 01:06.158
Peter, it's Carol,
your sister-in-law.

01:06.226 --> 01:07.799
Carol, how the
hell are you?

01:07.867 --> 01:10.543
Not too good.
Jason left me.

01:10.611 --> 01:13.020
(choking up): It's going
to be my ninth divorce.

01:13.088 --> 01:14.527
Oh, you know,
just hanging out.

01:14.595 --> 01:15.716
Peter, give me the phone.

01:15.784 --> 01:18.126
Carol, hi.
What's up?

01:18.194 --> 01:20.453
Oh, my God, that's terrible.

01:20.504 --> 01:23.582
Well, you know, Carol sweetie,
we are always here for you.

01:23.649 --> 01:25.808
Boy, Carol's lucky
to have a sister like Lois.

01:25.876 --> 01:27.013
You're telling me.

01:27.081 --> 01:28.586
I always wanted a
brother or sister,

01:28.654 --> 01:30.243
but instead I
got a bro-ster.

01:30.328 --> 01:33.290
Hey, Peter, you want
to see my peginas?

01:33.359 --> 01:37.006
Uh... I-I...
I don't know.

01:40.571 --> 01:42.327
I'm just saying
that if you read somewhere

01:42.395 --> 01:44.052
that Gwen Stefani
was a grandmother,

01:44.103 --> 01:45.977
you might not even bat an eye.

01:46.061 --> 01:47.482
What the devil?
What's going on?

01:47.567 --> 01:49.123
Oh, I'm sorry, Stewie.

01:49.191 --> 01:51.048
Your Aunt Carol's going
to be staying with us

01:51.117 --> 01:53.140
for a little while, so she's
going to need your room.

01:53.208 --> 01:54.779
Who the hell
do you think you are?

01:54.848 --> 01:56.034
Stop it this instant!

01:56.103 --> 01:57.525
We're not going
to fit through there.

01:57.610 --> 02:00.002
Only circles can fit through
that rectangular doorway.

02:00.087 --> 02:02.178
Oh, my God,
it's going right through.

02:02.263 --> 02:04.237
I-I-I got to get better
at shapes.

02:05.977 --> 02:08.303
Hey, Stewie. You going to
sleep in here with us?

02:08.371 --> 02:10.179
Is this how you spend
your free time, fat-ass?

02:10.247 --> 02:12.505
Lying half-naked
on a bed in black socks

02:12.573 --> 02:13.910
at 11:30
in the morning?

02:13.978 --> 02:16.219
You want to play
with one of Daddy's socks?

02:16.272 --> 02:17.777
What... do I want to...
No, what...

02:17.845 --> 02:19.635
No, what... what an odd request.

02:19.704 --> 02:21.943
Ah, the first one's always hard.

02:22.012 --> 02:23.937
There we go.

02:24.021 --> 02:26.363
The second one's easier,
'cause... toes.

02:26.431 --> 02:27.653
Ah, come on, fella.

02:28.557 --> 02:30.331
That's it.

02:30.398 --> 02:31.785
(yells)

02:31.854 --> 02:33.243
There you go.

02:33.310 --> 02:35.217
Create an adventure
with that.

02:40.425 --> 02:42.063
Carol, you're here.

02:42.130 --> 02:43.502
Hi, Lois.

02:43.570 --> 02:45.778
Oh, it's so wonderful
to see you.

02:45.846 --> 02:47.418
QUAGMIRE:
Hey, need some help
with those bags?

02:47.486 --> 02:49.611
Ooh, well, yes.
Thank you so much.

02:49.679 --> 02:52.540
Oh, it's my pleasure, Lois's
recently divorced sister.

02:52.608 --> 02:54.215
When I heard you
were coming to town,

02:54.282 --> 02:55.620
I figured I should
introduce myself.

02:55.688 --> 02:57.093
(chuckles)
How you doing?

02:57.161 --> 02:59.268
Well, not so good.

02:59.336 --> 03:01.243
You may have heard
that my husband left me.

03:01.311 --> 03:03.102
Oh, I'm so depressed.

03:03.170 --> 03:06.550
I mean, it can't always be
my fault, right? Uh-huh.

03:06.618 --> 03:08.692
You know, at first I thought
what I needed Uh-huh.

03:08.760 --> 03:11.369
was the immediate loving physical
touch of a new man. Uh-huh, yeah.

03:11.438 --> 03:13.981
But now I know what I
really need is a friend

03:14.049 --> 03:16.626
to just sit and listen to my problems.
Mm-hmm, uh-huh, uh-huh.

03:16.693 --> 03:18.668
I don't think that's too much to ask.
No, uh...

03:18.736 --> 03:22.248
I mean, how come men always feel
like they can just use a woman?

03:22.316 --> 03:23.353
You know?
Yeah.

03:23.421 --> 03:24.960
I mean, we have feelings.
Uh-huh.

03:25.028 --> 03:26.668
We're people.
Uh-huh, right.

03:26.735 --> 03:28.876
It's like they all have the same mindset.
Mm-hmm, uh-huh.

03:28.944 --> 03:31.422
It's all about sex, sex, sex.
Uh-huh. Yeah.

03:31.490 --> 03:34.517
And there's so much
more to life than that.

03:34.586 --> 03:37.329
20 feet that way, please.

03:40.075 --> 03:42.449
Oh, it's so great
to see you kids.

03:42.517 --> 03:45.096
Here. I brought you
all gifts from Texas.

03:45.164 --> 03:48.342
Meg, this is a bar of soap
with a scorpion in it.

03:48.409 --> 03:51.622
Stewie, this is a lollipop
with a desert spider in it.

03:51.689 --> 03:55.471
And Chris, this is a comb
made out of an armadillo shell.

03:55.523 --> 03:57.632
Now, just don't use it
to comb your hair

03:57.701 --> 04:00.009
and make promises
to women you won't keep.

04:00.077 --> 04:01.715
I probably just won't use it.

04:01.782 --> 04:03.422
Well, if you all
don't mind,

04:03.490 --> 04:05.229
I'm going to freshen up
before dinner.

04:07.205 --> 04:09.482
Mom, she's so sad
and lonely.

04:09.549 --> 04:10.955
Look who the
(bleep)'s talking.

04:11.023 --> 04:12.427
I'm sorry, sweetheart.

04:12.495 --> 04:14.468
It-it's just that
she's my sister.

04:14.537 --> 04:16.377
No wonder her husbands
all left her.

04:16.445 --> 04:18.051
Well, that's what
her problem is.

04:18.118 --> 04:20.796
She has such low self-esteem
that every time a man shows her

04:20.864 --> 04:22.602
the slightest glimmer
of attention,

04:22.671 --> 04:24.411
she rushes
into something serious

04:24.479 --> 04:26.888
and gets her heart broken
like a teenage girl.

04:26.956 --> 04:29.130
Hey, Tyler.
You on your way to algebra?

04:29.198 --> 04:30.419
Yeah, I'll
see you there.

04:30.486 --> 04:32.310
(sobbing loudly)

04:36.112 --> 04:39.341
You're awful!

04:39.426 --> 04:42.001
<i>ANNOUNCER:
The preceding joke
was brought to you by: Men.</i>

04:42.069 --> 04:43.826
♪ Men ♪

04:43.894 --> 04:46.137
♪ We don't know what we did. ♪

04:49.518 --> 04:52.261
Lois, thanks again for
letting me stay here.

04:52.329 --> 04:53.902
You can't imagine
how it feels

04:53.970 --> 04:56.279
to have your heart
broken so many times.

04:56.347 --> 04:59.559
Aunt Carol, Mom says you'd
make a great Florida whore.

04:59.628 --> 05:00.965
What does that mean?

05:01.032 --> 05:03.542
(forced laughing)

05:03.610 --> 05:06.639
Oh, Chris, I said that
about Kate Hudson.

05:06.707 --> 05:09.334
You know, I always thought
I'd be a good Florida whore.

05:09.419 --> 05:14.003
Thanks to you, my hair stays
like this when we stop.

05:14.071 --> 05:15.542
(doorbell rings)

05:17.503 --> 05:19.578
Mayor West? What are
you doing here?

05:19.645 --> 05:21.083
I'm being a rascal

05:21.151 --> 05:24.063
and ringing people's doorbells
and running away.

05:24.130 --> 05:25.770
Then, what are you
still doing here?

05:25.838 --> 05:28.815
It's my first house.
I'm not very good at this.

05:28.882 --> 05:30.221
Hello.

05:30.290 --> 05:32.900
Well, dot dot dot,
hello.

05:32.968 --> 05:34.740
Lois, aren't you
going to introduce me

05:34.807 --> 05:35.946
to your handsome
friend?

05:36.014 --> 05:37.184
Handsome?

05:37.252 --> 05:38.556
(sputtering excitedly)

05:38.624 --> 05:39.778
Wow!

05:39.846 --> 05:41.585
Oh, uh, sure.

05:41.654 --> 05:44.531
Carol, this is our
town's mayor, Adam West.

05:44.600 --> 05:46.540
Mayor West,
this is my sister Carol.

05:46.608 --> 05:50.324
Well, hubba-hubba ding-ding,
who invented this thing?

05:50.392 --> 05:52.499
(giggles)

05:52.566 --> 05:54.607
I bet you say that
to all the girls.

05:54.674 --> 05:57.989
Only the ones I'm about to ask
to Friendly's for a Fribble.

05:58.056 --> 06:01.035
Well, I don't really have
any plans this afternoon.

06:01.103 --> 06:03.663
Uh, Carol, given
your present situation,

06:03.731 --> 06:05.201
it's possible
that dating

06:05.270 --> 06:07.645
might not be such
a good idea right now.

06:07.713 --> 06:10.557
Actually, Lois, I think
it's exactly what I need.

06:10.625 --> 06:12.534
Hooray, it's a date!

06:12.601 --> 06:15.479
But we mustn't let
the local nobleman find out.

06:15.547 --> 06:18.693
<i>Otherwise, he'll assert
his right of prima noctus.</i>

06:18.761 --> 06:20.148
Oh, no,
we're too late.

06:20.216 --> 06:23.060
Ah! I must bless this coupling
with my soggy English penis.

06:23.128 --> 06:26.292
Oh, no!

06:26.377 --> 06:29.004
Come hither, that I may
sire thee a dreary Brit!

06:32.653 --> 06:36.082
You ever wonder what
a seagull tastes like?

06:36.149 --> 06:38.324
You know,
I actually have.

06:38.376 --> 06:40.468
I used to wonder,
but now I know.

06:40.519 --> 06:41.807
And it's not good.

06:41.859 --> 06:43.346
It's great!

06:43.398 --> 06:45.907
Wow, Adam. I just
have to tell you,

06:45.992 --> 06:48.216
I'm... I'm having
a terrific time.

06:48.301 --> 06:50.977
Me, too, Carol.
That was a great idea you had,

06:51.045 --> 06:53.055
about walking parallel
to the water.

06:53.106 --> 06:55.163
It makes for a longer walk.

06:55.230 --> 06:58.744
See? We're already making
each other better people.

06:58.796 --> 07:02.828
Carol, I want to make this
a night you'll never forget.

07:04.100 --> 07:06.043
(gunshot)
(groans)

07:06.111 --> 07:08.352
Oh, my.
Right?

07:23.467 --> 07:26.327
Oh, by the way, I should
tell you I've got AIDS.

07:26.395 --> 07:28.938
What? Yeah, they're right
over there waiting for me.

07:29.007 --> 07:30.814
Ready to go when you are, sir!

07:30.881 --> 07:33.192
Poor guys. They
both have AIDS.

07:33.243 --> 07:35.350
Not okay!

07:39.350 --> 07:42.311
(laughing heartily)

07:42.379 --> 07:44.855
Now let's hear
your fake laugh.

07:44.923 --> 07:46.330
How was your
date, you guys?

07:46.398 --> 07:48.757
Oh, Lois, it
was wonderful.

07:48.825 --> 07:50.262
I wish it didn't
have to end.

07:50.329 --> 07:51.851
Well, it's not over yet.

07:51.919 --> 07:53.140
What do you mean?

07:53.191 --> 07:55.852
You're right, Carol.
It was a wonderful date.

07:55.921 --> 07:57.860
And I want to have
a million more,

07:57.928 --> 08:00.806
every night of the week
for the rest of my life.

08:02.465 --> 08:03.801
Carol Yastrzemski--

08:03.869 --> 08:06.145
oh, please, let that
be your last name--

08:06.213 --> 08:08.121
will you marry me?

08:08.188 --> 08:09.862
What?
Oh, Adam.

08:09.929 --> 08:12.272
Yes, yes!
Adam, I will!

08:12.341 --> 08:13.794
Carol, what are
you doing?

08:13.862 --> 08:15.234
Are you crazy?

08:15.303 --> 08:16.539
You can't just jump
right back into...

08:16.607 --> 08:18.513
Lois, this is my life.

08:18.582 --> 08:20.187
And this is what I want.

08:20.255 --> 08:22.716
I will marry you,
Mayor Adam West!

08:22.783 --> 08:24.121
Oh, great!

08:24.189 --> 08:26.933
I can't wait to tell
the gals at my singles group.

08:27.000 --> 08:29.879
Ah, come on. This is
the goal, isn't it?

08:33.914 --> 08:35.620
Carol, I'm really not sure

08:35.621 --> 08:37.795
you should be rushing into
another marriage right now.

08:37.893 --> 08:40.270
Well, Lois, I
appreciate your input,

08:40.338 --> 08:42.579
but I'm capable of
making my own decisions.

08:42.648 --> 08:43.968
Are you sure?

08:44.036 --> 08:45.408
I mean, look
at your track record.

08:45.476 --> 08:46.881
Nine divorces.

08:46.949 --> 08:48.655
Do you really enjoy being

08:48.723 --> 08:50.930
Carol Pewterschmidt-Johnson-
Carrington-Stone-O'Craggity-

08:50.998 --> 08:54.011
Canseco-Shteinholtz-
Washington-Proudfoot-Fong?

08:54.079 --> 08:56.420
Look, I know I have a bit
of a checkered past,

08:56.488 --> 08:59.100
but I believe in
love at first sight.

08:59.151 --> 09:01.558
Hey, Lois, I didn't flush.
I want you to come see it.

09:01.627 --> 09:02.797
Looks like the
Starbucks mermaid.

09:02.866 --> 09:05.074
Peter, Carol
is marrying Mayor West.

09:05.141 --> 09:06.079
You serious?

09:06.146 --> 09:07.651
That's right, Peter.

09:07.719 --> 09:09.995
Say hello to your
future brother-in-law.

09:10.062 --> 09:11.468
Brother?

09:11.536 --> 09:13.074
I don't know those other
two words, but brother?

09:13.142 --> 09:14.614
I'm going to have a brother?

09:14.682 --> 09:16.990
Well, what we're discussing
is whether or not marriage

09:17.058 --> 09:19.970
is a smart move for Carol
at this point in time.

09:20.038 --> 09:22.280
I was hoping you might
be able to weigh in.

09:22.348 --> 09:23.853
I'm going to have a brother!

09:23.922 --> 09:25.811
Hey Adam, want to
go get in a fight

09:25.879 --> 09:27.970
over the rules of a
game we made up? Yep!

09:28.993 --> 09:30.530
PETER:
Five points. I win.

09:30.598 --> 09:32.372
MAYOR WEST:
That's not fair.
You weren't touching the bush.

09:32.440 --> 09:35.585
PETER:
I was too touching the bush...
Oh, cool, this rock has mica.

09:35.654 --> 09:36.858
MAYOR WEST:
Let me see!

09:36.927 --> 09:38.332
PETER:
Don't touch it.
It's worth money!

09:40.607 --> 09:41.946
I didn't know
you rode bikes.

09:41.998 --> 09:43.786
<i>I didn't know
you rode bikes.</i>

09:43.837 --> 09:45.209
Let's go butts-up
and fast.

09:47.186 --> 09:48.976
Oh, no!
Patch of sand.

09:53.880 --> 09:55.537
Oh, my God!
Are you all right?

09:55.588 --> 09:58.147
I'm fine. Let's just
keep riding bikes.

09:58.215 --> 10:00.005
Are you sure? That was
a wicked wipeout.

10:00.074 --> 10:03.336
(trying not to cry):
I'm fine. Can we just keep
riding bikes, please?

10:07.152 --> 10:09.160
Thanks for coming
to dinner, Lois.

10:09.228 --> 10:11.335
I know you have mixed
feelings about this.

10:11.403 --> 10:12.909
I was worried
you'd say no.

10:12.978 --> 10:16.225
Actually, I was worried you'd
say "Beetlejuice" three times.

10:16.292 --> 10:18.467
'Cause if you say
"Beetlejuice" three times,

10:18.535 --> 10:19.638
then Beetle...

10:19.706 --> 10:22.282
Oh, that was a close call.

10:22.350 --> 10:23.655
Oh, Carol.

10:23.723 --> 10:26.534
I would never miss any
of your meet-the-fianc├й dinners.

10:26.602 --> 10:28.142
This is the tenth one.

10:28.210 --> 10:29.781
Oh, Lois.

10:29.849 --> 10:32.024
Well, Carol, I'm just saying
it's a big decision.

10:32.092 --> 10:33.530
Life is full of big decisions.

10:33.598 --> 10:35.003
Like deciding whether or not

10:35.071 --> 10:36.409
you're going to have time
to masturbate.

10:36.476 --> 10:38.451
Peter, I'm going to run
and pick up Stewie.

10:38.519 --> 10:40.125
I'll be back in five
or ten minutes.

10:40.193 --> 10:41.665
Well, which is it?

10:41.734 --> 10:43.304
It's like I told you, Lois.

10:43.372 --> 10:45.079
This time it's different.

10:45.147 --> 10:48.526
Adam is the most kind and
attentive man I've ever known.

10:48.595 --> 10:50.099
Oh, that's interesting.

10:50.167 --> 10:53.078
'Cause I remember you saying
the same exact thing about Evan.

10:53.147 --> 10:54.552
Isn't that right, Evan?

10:54.620 --> 10:57.799
Yeah. Yeah, I do remember
her saying that about me.

10:57.867 --> 10:59.457
Evan. What are
you doing here?

10:59.524 --> 11:00.828
I brought him.

11:00.895 --> 11:03.104
Lois, you didn't tell me
you were bringing Evan here.

11:03.171 --> 11:04.913
We bonded over
the Red Sox one time,

11:04.981 --> 11:07.106
and now he's got to bring it up
every time I see him.

11:07.174 --> 11:08.628
How about those
Red Sox, Peter?

11:08.697 --> 11:10.619
Yeah, how about 'em, Evan?
They exist.

11:10.687 --> 11:12.060
How about
last season, huh?

11:12.128 --> 11:13.598
Yep.
How about next season?

11:13.666 --> 11:14.938
We'll see.

11:15.006 --> 11:16.778
Ho ho, I'm going
to hold you to that.

11:16.847 --> 11:19.491
Look, I see what you're
trying to do, Lois.

11:19.558 --> 11:21.801
And then there was your
second husband, Randall,

11:21.869 --> 11:23.273
who you called
your soul mate.

11:23.341 --> 11:24.931
Oh, God, not that guy.

11:24.998 --> 11:27.273
He always comes bearing
really inconvenient gifts.

11:27.341 --> 11:28.712
Hey, you guys.

11:28.780 --> 11:31.758
Peter, I brought you some
saltwater tropical fish.

11:31.827 --> 11:33.634
Now, you got to feed
'em every two hours,

11:33.702 --> 11:35.007
and I fed 'em
an hour ago.

11:35.075 --> 11:36.546
Uh, all right.
You got the food?

11:36.614 --> 11:37.902
No, I don't
have the food.

11:37.970 --> 11:39.575
You get that at a
tropical fish store.

11:39.642 --> 11:41.449
It's 9:30 at night.

11:41.518 --> 11:42.923
Well, you better
get going.

11:42.991 --> 11:45.015
Son of a bitch.

11:45.082 --> 11:47.559
And then there was your
third husband, Doggie Daddy,

11:47.626 --> 11:48.932
but you couldn't
make it work

11:48.999 --> 11:51.325
'cause he had that son
from a previous marriage.

11:51.393 --> 11:53.768
Hey, Carol.
Oh, hey.

11:53.836 --> 11:55.275
Just want
to let you know,

11:55.342 --> 11:57.449
Auggie's all grown up now,
out of the house.

11:57.518 --> 11:59.392
So... you know...

11:59.459 --> 12:01.635
Anyway, I'm around.

12:01.704 --> 12:04.111
This is really insensitive
of you, Lois.

12:04.179 --> 12:06.355
Do you have any idea
how difficult it is

12:06.423 --> 12:07.726
for me to see
my ex-husbands?

12:07.796 --> 12:09.568
That's my point, Carol.

12:09.636 --> 12:11.845
This is the kind of pain
I'm trying to spare you

12:11.913 --> 12:13.451
from experiencing
all over again.

12:13.519 --> 12:16.498
Why can't you just let me be
happy with the man I love?

12:16.566 --> 12:20.815
Carol, if you marry him, you're
just going to get hurt again.

12:20.883 --> 12:22.222
Is that what you want?

12:22.289 --> 12:24.499
Well... no.

12:24.566 --> 12:26.255
Look at these men.

12:26.323 --> 12:28.063
Are you psychologically able

12:28.131 --> 12:29.988
to deal with this pain
a tenth time?

12:30.056 --> 12:32.432
Oh, my God.

12:32.499 --> 12:34.542
Lois, you're right.

12:34.609 --> 12:36.767
Adam... I'm sorry.

12:36.834 --> 12:38.340
I can't marry you.

12:38.408 --> 12:39.762
What?

12:39.830 --> 12:41.470
B-B-B-But, Carol...

12:41.537 --> 12:44.801
I'm so sorry, Adam.
(sobbing)

12:46.458 --> 12:50.106
(sobbing)

12:52.115 --> 12:54.860
I think you sh...
should go now, Lois.

12:54.928 --> 12:56.298
I... I can't.

12:56.366 --> 12:57.705
My husband took the car.

13:00.970 --> 13:02.843
Hey... no, yeah.
No, I know it's late.

13:02.911 --> 13:04.264
I just need some
tropical fish food.

13:05.756 --> 13:07.714
Come on, where the hell
am I supposed to go?!

13:07.783 --> 13:09.036
You're in there!

13:10.593 --> 13:12.066
Don't worry, fish.

13:12.133 --> 13:13.689
I'm going to take care of you,

13:13.757 --> 13:16.250
'cause I know one day
you'll do the same for me.

13:16.317 --> 13:19.110
I'm sorry, but your friend
doesn't have insurance.

13:19.196 --> 13:22.224
I don't care what it costs.
Just make this man well again.

13:22.292 --> 13:23.832
Thank you, fish.

13:27.568 --> 13:31.383
MAN (over TV):
We now return to Germany's most
popular late-night talk show,

13:31.398 --> 13:33.304
<i>Still Awake
with Otto von Stade.</i>

13:33.372 --> 13:35.615
Some weather we're having.

13:35.683 --> 13:38.294
Okay.
I was recently in Munich.

13:38.362 --> 13:40.568
Did you ever notice how
the government buildings there

13:40.637 --> 13:42.410
are quite well-maintained?

13:42.478 --> 13:44.218
Okay.
We have no guest.

13:44.285 --> 13:45.522
Good night.

13:45.590 --> 13:48.034
Oh, how you
holding up, Carol?

13:48.101 --> 13:49.474
Not so good.

13:49.542 --> 13:52.302
Look, I know you're
depressed about Mayor West,

13:52.370 --> 13:55.214
but I promise you made
the right decision.

13:55.281 --> 13:56.586
Just give yourself
some time,

13:56.654 --> 13:58.829
and I'm sure you'll feel
much better about it.

13:58.897 --> 14:01.272
STEWIE:
She says that to Meg a lot,
so that's not good.

14:01.341 --> 14:02.613
You're probably right.

14:02.680 --> 14:04.454
(sighs) Oh, well.

14:04.522 --> 14:07.165
I guess I just better
go call all my friends

14:07.232 --> 14:09.340
and tell them
the wedding is off.

14:09.408 --> 14:11.297
(knocking)

14:13.641 --> 14:15.150
Oh, hi, Mayor West.

14:15.217 --> 14:16.353
Hi, Lois.

14:16.421 --> 14:18.730
I just wanted to return
Peter's Bedazzler.

14:18.798 --> 14:23.080
Tell him I never even
opened the box.

14:23.149 --> 14:24.352
Wait, Mayor West.

14:24.404 --> 14:26.260
Okay, I might have
opened the box.

14:26.328 --> 14:27.451
But listen,

14:27.519 --> 14:30.379
could you also say good-bye
to Carol for me?

14:30.447 --> 14:32.555
Good-bye?
Where are you going?

14:32.623 --> 14:35.099
As of today,
I'm stepping down as mayor

14:35.167 --> 14:37.073
and leaving Quahog forever.

14:37.140 --> 14:38.346
Because of Carol?

14:38.413 --> 14:39.818
That's right.

14:39.886 --> 14:42.632
I've decided to move to Alaska
and become an Eskimo.

14:42.699 --> 14:44.137
Alaska?

14:44.206 --> 14:45.912
But that's so far away.

14:45.979 --> 14:47.317
And the summers are so short.

14:48.841 --> 14:49.927
Yay.
Summer's here.

14:49.978 --> 14:51.684
Awesome.

14:51.770 --> 14:53.410
Best summer ever.
Let's stay friends.

14:53.477 --> 14:55.150
I lost my virginity.
Yeah, right. To who?

14:55.202 --> 14:57.041
You don't know her.
I met her at camp.

15:00.455 --> 15:02.179
Peter, what are you doing?

15:02.247 --> 15:04.138
I'm running away.
Why?

15:04.205 --> 15:06.113
Because you took away
my brother!

15:06.181 --> 15:07.753
You ruined my life!

15:07.821 --> 15:09.393
Adam and I were going
to be brothers forever,

15:09.462 --> 15:10.834
and now he's gone!

15:10.902 --> 15:13.645
All you got there is toys
and cans of tuna fish.

15:13.713 --> 15:15.953
Well, I got to eat.
And I got to have fun.

15:16.022 --> 15:17.327
Peter, you're just
acting out.

15:17.395 --> 15:18.734
No, I'm not.
I'm acting in.

15:18.801 --> 15:20.506
Okay, now you're just saying
the opposite of what I say.

15:20.575 --> 15:22.398
No, I'm not. I'm saying
the same as what you say.

15:22.467 --> 15:23.386
Peter.
Lois.

15:23.453 --> 15:24.859
God.
Allah.

15:24.926 --> 15:27.637
Look, I'm not sacrificing
my sister's happiness

15:27.705 --> 15:29.277
so you can have a playmate.

15:29.345 --> 15:31.051
She and Mayor West
never would've made it.

15:31.119 --> 15:32.591
Oh, yeah? Well, nobody
believed we'd make it,

15:32.676 --> 15:34.014
and look at us now.

15:34.065 --> 15:35.939
I drink, and you use
sex as a weapon.

15:36.007 --> 15:38.182
That seems to me like a
successful New England marriage.

15:38.249 --> 15:39.620
Oh, come on, Peter.

15:39.688 --> 15:41.662
My parents may have been
against our relationship,

15:41.730 --> 15:43.470
but a lot of people
supported us.

15:43.538 --> 15:44.476
Oh, yeah? Like who?

15:44.543 --> 15:46.783
Well, like... like Carol.

15:46.869 --> 15:48.725
God, if it wasn't
for her support,

15:48.794 --> 15:51.990
I don't know that I would've had
the confidence to marry you.

15:52.058 --> 15:54.315
And now that she
needs my support,

15:54.400 --> 15:56.341
here I am abandoning her.

15:56.409 --> 15:57.815
Oh, my God.

15:57.866 --> 16:00.140
Peter, I've made
a terrible mistake.

16:00.208 --> 16:01.647
Well, that's what
I tried to tell you,

16:01.714 --> 16:03.990
but you were as stubborn as
a pigeon at an ad agency.

16:04.058 --> 16:05.714
(all talking)

16:05.781 --> 16:10.552
Guys, guys, guys, guys, guys,
these are all good ideas,

16:10.620 --> 16:12.827
but I think what people
really get excited about

16:12.895 --> 16:14.652
is dropped
museum popcorn.

16:14.719 --> 16:16.995
But this is an ad campaign
for a Nissan SUV.

16:17.063 --> 16:18.301
How does that possibly...?

16:18.368 --> 16:20.879
Phil, Phil, Phil, Phil.

16:20.946 --> 16:23.055
I was put in charge
of this meeting.

16:23.122 --> 16:26.300
And we're going with
dropped museum popcorn.

16:33.131 --> 16:35.556
I'd like one ticket
to Alaska, please.

16:35.624 --> 16:36.728
One-way.

16:46.220 --> 16:48.763
(sighs)

16:48.831 --> 16:50.637
(tires squealing)

16:54.019 --> 16:57.282
Has the... has the 4:10 flight
to Alaska left yet?

16:57.350 --> 16:58.587
I'm afraid so.

16:58.672 --> 17:00.094
That... that's it
right there.

17:04.580 --> 17:07.391
(sobbing):
Oh, no, we're too late.

17:07.458 --> 17:08.930
I'm sorry, Carol.

17:08.997 --> 17:12.028
But if it helps, you're not the
first person to get a bum deal.

17:12.096 --> 17:14.772
Mr. Edison, we're going
to use your invention

17:14.841 --> 17:15.976
to power the world.

17:16.044 --> 17:18.052
And Mr. Tesla,
we're going to use yours

17:18.120 --> 17:19.675
in the background
of Frankenstein movies.

17:19.743 --> 17:21.114
Aw, I wanted that one.

17:24.934 --> 17:27.276
I am so sorry, you guys.

17:27.343 --> 17:28.981
This is all my fault.

17:29.049 --> 17:30.589
It's okay, Lois.

17:30.657 --> 17:33.232
I know you tried your best
to make things right.

17:33.300 --> 17:35.776
Well, since I ain't got no
brother to play army guys with,

17:35.843 --> 17:37.384
I might as well go
to the Clam.

17:39.845 --> 17:42.221
Hey, Quagmire, it's me.
Meet me at the Clam in 20.

17:42.288 --> 17:44.162
That sounds great, Peter,
but I can't right now.

17:44.230 --> 17:45.635
Oh, crap. Why not?

17:45.703 --> 17:46.706
'Cause I'm working.

17:46.774 --> 17:48.096
I'm flying a plane to Alaska.

17:48.163 --> 17:49.787
Alaska? What the hell?

17:49.854 --> 17:51.678
I never even heard
of this place an hour ago,

17:51.762 --> 17:52.597
and now everybody's
flying there.

17:52.665 --> 17:53.770
What did you say?

17:53.838 --> 17:55.527
Quagmire's flying
to Alaska?

17:55.595 --> 17:57.685
Peter, that's the plane
Mayor West is on!

17:57.754 --> 17:59.727
Oh, I get what
you're driving at.

17:59.794 --> 18:02.321
If Carol can't have
Mayor West, no one can.

18:02.390 --> 18:04.065
Quagmire, I want you
to crash that plane.

18:04.132 --> 18:05.301
No survivors,
you hear me?

18:05.370 --> 18:07.177
Peter,
for God sakes, no.

18:07.244 --> 18:09.084
Tell him
to turn the plane around.

18:09.152 --> 18:10.490
Oh, hey, listen,
Quagmire,

18:10.558 --> 18:12.263
turn the plane around
and bring it back here.

18:12.331 --> 18:13.670
Peter, I can't do that.

18:13.737 --> 18:15.176
The FAA would be
all over my ass.

18:21.471 --> 18:23.512
Um, hey Quagmire,
you know that stray cat

18:23.579 --> 18:25.736
we got in our neighborhood?
The pregnant one?

18:25.804 --> 18:27.612
Yep, she's having
her kittens right now.

18:27.681 --> 18:29.570
(gasps)
Oh, no way.

18:29.638 --> 18:30.976
Yeah, making all those
high-pitched

18:31.045 --> 18:32.767
"mew mew" noises and everything.

18:32.818 --> 18:34.709
I love them already.

18:34.776 --> 18:37.052
Yeah, but Brian's been eyeing
them pretty good. What?

18:37.120 --> 18:38.925
Yeah, and I got to go
to work now.

18:38.994 --> 18:40.634
So I hope he's not, like,
hungry or nothing.

18:41.790 --> 18:43.060
This is your captain speaking.

18:43.144 --> 18:44.501
We've discovered a shoe bomb
on board.

18:44.569 --> 18:46.157
We're going to have to return
to the terminal.

18:57.440 --> 19:00.635
Hello, Alaska!

19:00.720 --> 19:01.723
Adam!

19:01.790 --> 19:03.028
Carol?

19:08.803 --> 19:11.414
I don't know
what I was thinking.

19:11.499 --> 19:13.020
I made nine mistakes,

19:13.089 --> 19:15.765
and I was afraid that I was
going to make another one.

19:15.833 --> 19:18.360
And you know what?
I almost did.

19:18.427 --> 19:21.907
Adam, I want to spend
the rest of my life with you.

19:21.975 --> 19:23.850
Oh, Carol.

19:23.918 --> 19:25.758
Nothing would
make me happier.

19:33.625 --> 19:36.301
Hey, you know, when it's
your turn to say, "I do,"

19:36.370 --> 19:38.378
you should say, "I do
the Dew," and then...

19:38.446 --> 19:40.285
and then we both pull
out a Mountain Dew,

19:40.353 --> 19:41.690
and then I'll-I'll...
and then I do,

19:41.759 --> 19:43.500
like, a radical skateboard
jump off the altar.

19:43.568 --> 19:45.406
Well, obviously, yeah.

19:45.474 --> 19:48.654
("Bridal Chorus" playing)

19:52.973 --> 19:54.947
Good luck.

19:55.015 --> 19:56.421
I hope you like
weird boobs.

20:01.107 --> 20:03.582
Dearly beloved,
we are gathered here

20:03.651 --> 20:07.032
to join this man and
this woman in holy matrimony.

20:07.100 --> 20:09.677
The bride and groom
have written their own vows.

20:09.745 --> 20:12.821
Adam, from the first
day I met you,

20:12.890 --> 20:15.365
I knew that we were
meant to be together.

20:15.434 --> 20:17.074
I know I've said
that before--

20:17.142 --> 20:18.346
a lot of times--

20:18.414 --> 20:20.322
and it's always
blown up in my face.

20:20.389 --> 20:21.359
QUAGMIRE:
Giggity.

20:21.427 --> 20:22.597
But this time
it's different.

20:22.665 --> 20:24.504
I know it's
going to work.

20:24.573 --> 20:25.710
I love you...

20:25.777 --> 20:28.187
and I'll be proud
to be your wife.

20:28.254 --> 20:29.782
That was lovely.

20:29.872 --> 20:30.807
Adam?

20:31.980 --> 20:34.524
Milk. Butter. Cat food.

20:34.592 --> 20:37.201
Those new potato chips
in the snack aisle.

20:37.270 --> 20:40.817
Beer. Deodorant.
Garbage bags. Toothpaste.

20:40.885 --> 20:42.792
Adam, I think that's
your shopping list.

20:42.860 --> 20:44.834
No. These are my vows.

20:44.902 --> 20:47.579
They're all the reasons
why I'll always love you.

20:47.647 --> 20:49.319
Oh, Adam.

20:49.387 --> 20:51.528
I now pronounce you
husband and wife.

20:51.597 --> 20:53.739
You may kiss the bride.

20:56.434 --> 20:59.011
Oh, Peter, I'm so happy
for the both of them.

20:59.079 --> 21:02.098
Hey, I just realized...
I have a sister.

21:04.400 --> 21:05.846
WEST: Ha. Stand much?

21:05.847 --> 22:06.847
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru